&lt;h1&gt;


  A Website Redesign Is a URL Migration: Build the Redirect Map Before Launch
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A redesigned service website can pass a visual review while still breaking an important contract: existing addresses should continue to lead people to useful information.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Those addresses may appear in search results, customer emails, advertising, proposals, saved bookmarks, and links from other websites. Replacing navigation does not update those references.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Website redesign redirect mapping belongs near the start of the build. It creates an explicit record of what each important URL becomes and prevents migration decisions being improvised during launch.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a small business site, the process can be straightforward. The challenge is usually ownership and completeness, rather than a need for complicated software.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Inventory the addresses that matter
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Start with the current sitemap and the URLs available from the content management system. Supplement them with pages appearing in search performance data, analytics landing-page reports, advertising destinations, and known customer-facing resources.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Include useful documents and other linked assets where their addresses will change. A PDF linked from a proposal can matter to the business even if it is not part of the main navigation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Keep the inventory in a versioned file. Suggested fields include old URL, content purpose, proposed action, destination, reason, owner, and test result. Preserve the original values so the team can distinguish a deliberate decision from an accidental omission.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Do not assume a low-traffic URL has no value. It may support an important sales conversation or receive visitors only during a particular part of the year. Ask the business before deleting material whose role is unclear.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Choose an action for each URL
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Useful actions include keeping the address, updating its content, moving it to a new address, merging it into a genuinely related page, or retiring it when no appropriate replacement exists.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Keeping a useful URL can be the simplest option. A new visual design does not require changing every path.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When content moves, identify the closest relevant destination. If several old pages become one stronger page, explain what information is being consolidated so reviewers can assess whether the mapping makes sense.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a retired page without a relevant replacement, a proper not-found response may be more appropriate than sending the visitor to an unrelated offer. The decision should reflect what happened to the content, not a desire to make every request return a successful page.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Make the map readable to both teams
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The developer needs an implementable mapping. The business needs to understand whether important information survives. A small table can serve both purposes:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Old path&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Action&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Destination&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Reason&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;/services/&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Keep&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;/services/&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Existing overview remains useful&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;/old-repair-page/&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Move&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;/repair-services/&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Same service with a revised address&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;/legacy-guide/&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Merge&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;/service-planning/&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Relevant guidance is retained in the new page&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;/expired-offer/&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Retire&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;None&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Offer ended and has no equivalent replacement&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These are illustrative paths, not recommendations for a particular live website. The important feature is that each row explains the intended content relationship.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Review the map before implementation. That gives the business an opportunity to catch missing services while there is still time to revise the structure.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Implement redirects deliberately
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/site-move-with-url-changes" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Google’s site-move documentation&lt;/a&gt; recommends permanent server-side redirects for moved URLs, directing them to relevant destinations and avoiding unnecessary redirect chains. It also calls for updating internal links and URL annotations as part of the move.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Translate the approved map into the mechanism appropriate for the hosting environment. Keep the configuration under the same review discipline as other production changes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Test the final destination rather than stopping at the first redirect response. A redirect can exist while pointing to another redirect, an error page, an unrelated page, or an address with an inconsistent canonical reference.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Take care with broad pattern rules. A convenient rule that catches many paths may also affect URLs that should behave differently. Test representative matches and exceptions before relying on it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Test behaviour, not just status codes
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A useful migration check asks several questions. Does the old URL resolve according to the map? Does the destination load the intended content? Can a visitor complete the next task? Are the new page’s internal links current?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For retained pages, confirm that the content remains present and that the new layout has not accidentally hidden essential information. For merged pages, confirm that the relevant explanation actually made it into the new page.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Keep redirect tests separate from form and booking tests, but include both in the launch review. A migration can preserve every URL while breaking the enquiry route.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Record failures with enough detail to reproduce them: requested URL, observed result, expected result, and the environment tested. That is more actionable than marking the whole website “SEO checked.”&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Plan the launch and monitoring responsibilities
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Assign someone to implement changes and someone to verify them. Make clear who can approve a correction if a missing destination is discovered after launch.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Retain a copy of the pre-launch map and a record of the deployed version. If the live configuration differs from the approved file, explain why.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;After release, inspect important pages and actual entry routes. Review not-found requests, enquiry failures, and search performance alongside the deployment record. A problem affecting a valuable service page deserves attention even if the overall website appears healthy.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Avoid attributing every traffic change to a single redirect decision. Timing, content changes, search demand, and other marketing activity can also influence results. Use the map and launch record to narrow the investigation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Treat the URL map as a reviewable project asset
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Keep migration decisions alongside the work they affect. A spreadsheet or a simple structured file can be enough, provided the team knows which version is authoritative and how changes are approved.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Add an explicit reason when a destination differs from the original plan. If two services are combined during content review, update the map before the redirect configuration is generated. Otherwise the design, content, and technical implementation can quietly drift apart.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The owner of a row does not need to be the person implementing it. A service lead may confirm that the replacement page is appropriate, while a developer handles the response behaviour. Separating those responsibilities helps avoid treating a technical success as proof that the content decision was correct.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Where search performance informs the review, include the relevant evidence without using it as the only decision rule. &lt;a href="https://bizfront.ca/search-engine-optimization/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Bizfront’s SEO services&lt;/a&gt; emphasize the pages and searches that matter to the business. A low-volume page can still deserve attention if it serves a valuable, specific customer need.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Define a small set of migration invariants
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Write down the conditions the launch must preserve. For a typical service website, these might include keeping the agreed priority pages reachable, retaining essential service information, and ensuring that existing marketing destinations continue to lead to relevant offers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Make each condition testable. “Protect SEO” is too broad for an acceptance check. “The old repairs URL resolves to the approved repairs destination, which contains the agreed service explanation and a working enquiry route” is something a reviewer can verify.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Use these conditions to prioritize testing when the inventory is large. Every mapping still needs review, but a broken route into a priority service may require more immediate attention than a low-value archival resource.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For projects involving &lt;a href="https://bizfront.ca/website-design-calgary/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Bizfront’s website planning and development&lt;/a&gt;, agree on these migration conditions as part of the scope. The service page describes the overall planning-to-launch process; the specific URL requirements should be made explicit for the website being replaced.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Test an old link as a customer would use it
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Automated checks can identify response behaviour, but the customer’s task continues after the redirect. Open representative old links in a browser and review the resulting page with its actual content.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Start with links used in advertising, proposals, sales emails, and important external references. Check the page title, the service explanation, and the next step. If a visitor was promised a guide, a redirect to a general homepage may be technically functional while failing the expectation created by the original link.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Also test links with the query parameters your marketing uses. Confirm that the intended page still loads and that the application handles those parameters according to the agreed design. Do not assume all tracking or campaign URLs behave identically to the clean path.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Record the result in the map or a linked test log. The combination of automated status checks and a small number of realistic customer journeys provides a clearer picture than either alone.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Keep commercial context in technical prioritization
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developers may see a set of URLs where the business sees different services, customer groups, and opportunities. Ask the project owner to identify those distinctions before launch.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The service-page work described in &lt;a href="https://bizfront.ca/case-studies/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Bizfront’s client case studies&lt;/a&gt; illustrates why this matters. A page may exist because the business wants more of a particular type of work, not simply because the site needed another navigation item.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That context can influence the order of verification and the response to a fault. If a priority advertising destination is wrong, the team may need to correct the page and review active campaign links immediately. If an outdated informational resource has no appropriate replacement, the decision may be to retire it deliberately.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Technical consistency still matters across the whole site. Commercial context helps the team recognize which failures interrupt the most important journeys and communicate their consequences clearly.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Prepare for corrections without losing the decision history
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Launch reviews sometimes reveal omissions even after careful planning. Decide how corrections will be recorded, reviewed, deployed, and retested so the map remains an accurate description of the live site.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Avoid making a series of undocumented one-off changes in the hosting interface. When practical, retain the relevant configuration and the reason for the correction in the project’s normal change process.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A clear record reduces the chance that a later update reintroduces the problem. It also gives the next person maintaining the website enough context to understand why a redirect exists instead of removing it because the original page is no longer visible in the content system.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Make migration part of the project scope
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The URL plan connects design decisions to existing customer and search journeys. It is easier to manage when page structure, content, and technical implementation are discussed together.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://bizfront.ca/website-design-calgary/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Bizfront’s website design process&lt;/a&gt; describes planning the messaging and pages before the build and testing before launch. When a project replaces an existing site, teams should explicitly agree how URL inventory, migration, and monitoring fit into that scope.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A redirect map is a small deliverable with a clear purpose: every important old address receives a deliberate outcome. Build it while the structure is being decided, review it while changes are still inexpensive, and use it as a testable record when the new website goes live.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Short answer:&lt;/strong&gt; Retry failed webhook delivery only for plausibly transient failures, with a stable event ID, exponential backoff with jitter, a hard attempt cap, and a dead-letter record that preserves the evidence.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A useful decision rule for failed webhook delivery is this: retry only when the receiver may succeed later, assign every attempt a stable event identity, and record enough evidence to reconcile the final outcome. In a property-management system, a failure alert can concern a tenant-impacting incident, but the alert itself must not become an unbounded source of duplicate notifications or unexplained delivery cost.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Bounded beats endless.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The design below treats delivery as an at-least-once transport with an exactly-once accounting mindset. The receiver may observe duplicates; the sender must make duplicates detectable, bounded, and auditable.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What must a retry preserve?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The invariants are more important than the backoff formula. A delivery record needs an immutable event ID, a destination identifier, an attempt number, the response class, and timestamps for scheduled, started, and completed work. The payload should carry the event ID as an idempotency key, while the delivery ledger remains the source of truth for cost attribution.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A timeout does not prove that the receiver failed to process the message. It proves only that the sender did not obtain a response within its deadline. Retrying that request can therefore produce a duplicate side effect. The consumer should deduplicate by event ID, and the producer should never mark a timed-out attempt as a confirmed success.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The failure boundary is equally concrete. A 2xx response is a successful HTTP response; a 4xx response generally indicates that changing the request is required, so an automatic retry needs an explicit policy; a 5xx response or transport error is a candidate for retry. Retry-After can provide a delay when the receiver sends one, but a client still needs a maximum delay and a maximum attempt count. Six attempts is a reasonable starting policy for a low-latency alert, but it is an operational choice, not an HTTP rule.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is the decision record I would attach to the alert-delivery service:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Option&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Use it when&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Main cost or risk&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Immediate repeated requests&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A transient dependency has a very short recovery window&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Load spikes and duplicate side effects become more likely&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Exponential backoff with jitter&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Receiver overload and temporary network failure are plausible&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Incidents take longer to surface as permanently failed&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Dead-letter queue after bounded attempts&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;An operator must inspect or replay evidence&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Queue retention and reconciliation work become part of operations&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;No retry for selected 4xx classes&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The request is invalid or unauthorized&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A configuration mistake remains visible and requires a fix&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For property-management alerts, the last two options usually belong together: bounded retries preserve a chance of delivery, and a dead-letter record preserves the evidence when that chance is exhausted.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  How should the critical path classify failure?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The worker should separate classification from scheduling. It should also persist the attempt before making the network call, because a process crash after the call and before the write is an ambiguity that cannot be repaired by optimistic logging later. A compact Go implementation can make those boundaries explicit:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight go"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;package&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;delivery&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="s"&gt;"context"&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="s"&gt;"errors"&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="s"&gt;"fmt"&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="s"&gt;"math/rand"&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="s"&gt;"net/http"&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="s"&gt;"time"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;var&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ErrPermanent&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;errors&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;New&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"permanent delivery failure"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;func&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;classify&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;int&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;200&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;300&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;400&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;500&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;fmt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Errorf&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"%w: HTTP %d"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ErrPermanent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;fmt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Errorf&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"retryable HTTP %d"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;func&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;nextDelay&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;attempt&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;int&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;retryAfter&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;time&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Duration&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;rand&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Rand&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;time&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Duration&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;retryAfter&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;retryAfter&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;base&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;time&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Second&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;time&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Duration&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="m"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;min&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;attempt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;6&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;))&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;base&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="m"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;time&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Duration&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Int63n&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;int64&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;base&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="m"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)))&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;func&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;min&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;b&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;int&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;int&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;b&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;b&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;func&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;deliver&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ctx&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;client&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Client&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;req&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Request&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;resp&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;client&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Do&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;req&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;WithContext&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ctx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;))&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;classify&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="m"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;defer&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;resp&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Close&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;classify&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;resp&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;StatusCode&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The snippet intentionally leaves persistence and queue choice behind interfaces. In production, &lt;code&gt;attempt_started&lt;/code&gt; should be durable before &lt;code&gt;deliver&lt;/code&gt; runs, and &lt;code&gt;attempt_completed&lt;/code&gt; should include the status or transport error. The scheduler then computes the next due time from the recorded attempt, rather than from process memory. A real implementation should also cap the body read, enforce a request deadline, and validate the destination policy before enqueueing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The jitter matters because a shared outage can release many workers at once. The exact constants are a policy choice, not a universal truth; they should be tested against receiver limits, alert urgency, and the cost of each attempt.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  How should handling retries for failed webhook delivery stop failure loops?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Ask whether the notification is merely informative or is part of an operational control loop. A missed informational alert can wait for an operator replay. A message that triggers lease, access, or payment workflow requires stronger consumer idempotency and a more conservative replay procedure. In both cases, the delivery service should retain the original event and its decision history rather than reconstructing a new event during replay.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The useful metrics are not just success rate. Track attempts by destination and event class, age of the oldest pending delivery, retry count, permanent-failure count, timeout count, and bytes sent. Those dimensions connect reliability to cost attribution: a noisy destination, oversized payload, or repeatedly failing tenant integration can be identified without charging every retry to an undifferentiated platform total. Logs should include the event ID, delivery ID, attempt number, decision, and correlation ID, while excluding secrets and unnecessary tenant data. OpenTelemetry describes logs as a signal that can carry structured context; that context is valuable only when its cardinality and retention are governed deliberately.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The trade-off is visible in the ledger: more attempts can improve delivery probability, while also increasing receiver load, duplicate exposure, retention, and attributable network work. A policy that cannot state which of those costs it accepts is incomplete.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An audit trail also needs a retention rule. Keep enough data to reconstruct why the alert was delivered, delayed, or abandoned, but avoid copying sensitive incident payloads into every attempt record. Store a content digest and immutable references where the operational policy permits it. That gives reconciliation a stable identity without turning observability data into a second customer database.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The rejected option is a universal retry loop: send the request, sleep, and repeat until it works. It is easy to write and difficult to operate. It can amplify a receiver outage, hide invalid credentials, create duplicate effects after timeouts, and make the cost of one original incident impossible to explain. It is valid only for a tightly bounded, idempotent operation whose receiver contract explicitly permits repetition, and even there it needs a deadline and an audit record.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A queue does not remove these responsibilities. It moves them into scheduling, retention, and replay semantics. The safest boundary is a finite state machine such as &lt;code&gt;pending -&amp;gt; delivering -&amp;gt; delivered&lt;/code&gt;, with &lt;code&gt;retry_scheduled&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;dead_lettered&lt;/code&gt; terminally visible states. A lease or fencing token prevents two workers from owning the same attempt, while an operator replay creates a new attempt linked to the same event ID.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The practical test is a failure-injection table: receiver returns 2xx, receiver returns 429 with Retry-After, receiver returns 401, receiver closes the connection after reading the body, DNS fails, the worker crashes after send, and the queue redelivers the job. For each case, specify the stored state, the next action, and the evidence available to reconcile it. If those answers are not deterministic, the retry policy is not ready for a customer-facing failure alert.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  References
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://opentelemetry.io/docs/concepts/signals/logs/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://opentelemetry.io/docs/concepts/signals/logs/&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9457" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9457&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6585" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6585&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When we start developing a new application, one of the first things we usually discuss is its architecture.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Should we use Clean Architecture? A modular monolith? A traditional layered architecture?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We define our projects, organize dependencies, establish some rules, and everything looks good.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But what happens after six months? Or after several developers have worked on the same solution?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;More importantly, &lt;strong&gt;who makes sure that the architecture we initially designed is still respected?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The compiler? Not really.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Our unit tests? Not necessarily.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Code reviews? Maybe, if someone notices the problem.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is something I've been thinking about for a while, especially with the increasing adoption of AI coding agents.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Let's take a practical example to understand the problem, and then see how we can address it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  When valid C# becomes an architectural problem
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Imagine we're developing an order management application using Clean Architecture.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Our solution contains the following projects:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;Shop.slnx
│
├── Shop.Domain
│   ├── Order.cs
│   └── IOrderRepository.cs
│
├── Shop.Application
│   └── OrderService.cs
│
├── Shop.Infrastructure
│   └── SqlOrderRepository.cs
│
└── Shop.Api
    └── Program.cs
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Nothing complicated here.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Domain layer contains our business model and abstractions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Application contains the use cases.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Infrastructure contains the technical implementations.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And API is our entry point and composition root.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;According to the Dependency Rule, dependencies should point inward. Domain should not know about Infrastructure, and Application should not directly depend on Infrastructure implementations.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Now, imagine that we're implementing a use case to confirm an order.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A developer writes the following code:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight csharp"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;using&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nn"&gt;Shop.Domain&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;using&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nn"&gt;Shop.Infrastructure&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;namespace&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nn"&gt;Shop.Application&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;public&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;sealed&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;class&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;OrderService&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;SqlOrderRepository&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;repository&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;public&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Task&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;ConfirmAsync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;Guid&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;orderId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;CancellationToken&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;cancellationToken&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="kt"&gt;var&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;order&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;repository&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;GetAsync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="n"&gt;orderId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="n"&gt;cancellationToken&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

        &lt;span class="n"&gt;order&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;Confirm&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;

        &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;repository&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;SaveAsync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="n"&gt;order&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="n"&gt;cancellationToken&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Assuming the project reference exists and the repository exposes these methods, this code is perfectly valid from the compiler's perspective.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It can compile successfully.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The unit tests can pass.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The feature can work exactly as expected.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So, what's the problem?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Well, our Application layer now directly depends on Infrastructure.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We have introduced an outward dependency that violates the architecture we initially agreed on.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And this is where things become interesting.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The compiler checks whether our code is valid C#. It doesn't know whether the dependencies respect our architectural decisions.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Of course, we could catch this during code review. But should we really depend entirely on code reviews to detect something that can be checked automatically?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I don't think so.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Let's refactor our code
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The problem is not the functionality itself. The problem is the dependency.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Instead of injecting &lt;code&gt;SqlOrderRepository&lt;/code&gt;, our Application layer should depend on an abstraction.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Let's start with our Domain project.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight csharp"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;namespace&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nn"&gt;Shop.Domain&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;public&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;interface&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;IOrderRepository&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;Task&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Order&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;GetAsync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;Guid&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;CancellationToken&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;cancellationToken&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="n"&gt;Task&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;SaveAsync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;Order&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;order&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;CancellationToken&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;cancellationToken&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;We can now change our Application service:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight csharp"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;using&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nn"&gt;Shop.Domain&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;namespace&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nn"&gt;Shop.Application&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;public&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;sealed&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;class&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;OrderService&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;IOrderRepository&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;repository&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;public&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Task&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;ConfirmAsync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;Guid&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;orderId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;CancellationToken&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;cancellationToken&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="kt"&gt;var&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;order&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;repository&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;GetAsync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="n"&gt;orderId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="n"&gt;cancellationToken&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

        &lt;span class="n"&gt;order&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;Confirm&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;

        &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;repository&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;SaveAsync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="n"&gt;order&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="n"&gt;cancellationToken&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;And let Infrastructure implement the abstraction:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight csharp"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;using&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nn"&gt;Shop.Domain&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;namespace&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nn"&gt;Shop.Infrastructure&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;public&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;sealed&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;class&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;SqlOrderRepository&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;IOrderRepository&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Persistence implementation&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Finally, we register the implementation in our API project:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight csharp"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;builder&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Services&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;AddScoped&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;IOrderRepository&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;SqlOrderRepository&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;There it is!&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Our Application layer no longer depends on Infrastructure.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We have restored the intended dependency direction.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But we haven't solved the original problem yet.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What prevents another developer from introducing the same violation tomorrow?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Nothing, unless we introduce a mechanism to enforce that boundary.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What if architectural rules were executable?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We already automate a lot of things in software development.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We use analyzers to enforce coding conventions, tests to validate behavior, and CI pipelines to verify our changes before merging.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Why shouldn't we do the same with architecture?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Imagine being able to declare the following rules:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Application can depend on Domain.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Infrastructure can depend on Domain.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Domain cannot depend on Application or Infrastructure.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Modules can only access another module's explicitly exposed contracts.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And then automatically validate those rules against our solution.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is the idea behind &lt;strong&gt;Architecture Guardian&lt;/strong&gt;, an open-source .NET tool I've been working on.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It allows us to describe architectural boundaries using a YAML policy and validate them through Roslyn analyzers, a CLI, and MCP tools for AI coding agents.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Let's see how it works.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Defining our architecture
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Instead of keeping our architecture rules only in documentation, we'll describe them in a file called &lt;code&gt;architecture.guardian.yaml&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For our Shop solution, the policy can look like this:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight yaml"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;version&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="na"&gt;modules&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="pi"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;Shop&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;projects&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="pi"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Shop.*/*.csproj"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="na"&gt;layers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="pi"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;Domain&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;projects&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="pi"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Shop.Domain/*.csproj"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="pi"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;Application&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;projects&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="pi"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Shop.Application/*.csproj"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;allowedDependencies&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="pi"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt;Domain&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="pi"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;Infrastructure&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;projects&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="pi"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Shop.Infrastructure/*.csproj"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;allowedDependencies&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="pi"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt;Domain&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="pi"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;Api&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;projects&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="pi"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Shop.Api/*.csproj"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;allowedDependencies&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="pi"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;Domain&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="pi"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;Application&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="pi"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;Infrastructure&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="na"&gt;rules&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;AG002&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;AG003&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="na"&gt;gate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;failOn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Here, each project belongs to a layer, and each layer has an explicit list of allowed dependencies.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Notice that Application can only depend on Domain.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So, if someone introduces a dependency from Application to Infrastructure, it will violate our policy.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The important point is that Architecture Guardian doesn't impose Clean Architecture on your application.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;You define your architecture. The tool verifies whether your code respects it.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, if we were using a different architectural style, we could define different boundaries and permissions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The policy should describe the architecture we've chosen, not an architecture selected by the tool.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Let's break our architecture again
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Remember our first implementation of &lt;code&gt;OrderService&lt;/code&gt;?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Let's introduce the same problem again.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight csharp"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;using&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nn"&gt;Shop.Infrastructure&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;namespace&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nn"&gt;Shop.Application&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;public&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;sealed&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;class&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;OrderService&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;SqlOrderRepository&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;repository&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// ...&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;And suppose we add the corresponding project reference.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Our application can still compile.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But now we can validate the architecture.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;First, install the Architecture Guardian CLI:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;dotnet tool &lt;span class="nb"&gt;install&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--global&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="se"&gt;\&lt;/span&gt;
  ArchitectureGuardian.Cli &lt;span class="se"&gt;\&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--version&lt;/span&gt; 0.3.0
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;After restoring the solution, we can run:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;archguard scan Shop.slnx &lt;span class="se"&gt;\&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--policy&lt;/span&gt; architecture.guardian.yaml &lt;span class="se"&gt;\&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--configuration&lt;/span&gt; Release &lt;span class="se"&gt;\&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--trusted&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;For a repository we've authorized for MSBuild evaluation, &lt;code&gt;--trusted&lt;/code&gt; allows the scan to proceed. It doesn't isolate untrusted build logic.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The scan can now detect our forbidden dependency and report an AG002 error.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An example diagnostic looks like this:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;Shop.Application/OrderService.cs:
error AG002:
Layer 'Application' must not depend on
layer 'Infrastructure'.
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Our code still compiles.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But our architecture validation fails.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's exactly what we wanted.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Instead of depending on someone noticing an architectural violation during a pull request, we now have an explicit rule that can be checked automatically.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What about a modular monolith?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So far, we've looked at a fairly simple layered architecture.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Let's take a slightly more interesting case.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Imagine we're developing a modular monolith containing three business modules:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;src/
├── Catalog/
│   ├── Catalog.Domain/
│   ├── Catalog.Application/
│   ├── Catalog.Contracts/
│   └── Catalog.Infrastructure/
│
├── Orders/
│   ├── Orders.Domain/
│   ├── Orders.Application/
│   └── Orders.Infrastructure/
│
└── Billing/
    ├── Billing.Contracts/
    ├── Billing.Application/
    └── Billing.Infrastructure/
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Orders needs to retrieve some product information from Catalog.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A developer finds the following class:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight csharp"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;namespace&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nn"&gt;Catalog.Infrastructure&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;public&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;sealed&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;class&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ProductRepository&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;public&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Task&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Product&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;GetAsync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Guid&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// ...&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Since the class is public, the developer decides to inject it into Orders:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight csharp"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;using&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nn"&gt;Catalog.Infrastructure&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;namespace&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nn"&gt;Orders.Application&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;public&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;sealed&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;class&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;CreateOrderHandler&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;ProductRepository&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;products&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// ...&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Again, the code can compile if the required references exist.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But we've introduced another problem.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Orders now knows about Catalog's internal persistence implementation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What happens if Catalog changes its storage technology?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What if we replace SQL Server with another persistence mechanism?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Why should Orders care about that?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It shouldn't.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is an example of an implementation detail leaking across module boundaries.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Refactoring through a public contract
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Let's expose an abstraction through &lt;code&gt;Catalog.Contracts&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight csharp"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;namespace&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nn"&gt;Catalog.Contracts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;public&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;interface&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;IProductReader&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;Task&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ProductDto&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;?&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;GetAsync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;Guid&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;productId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;CancellationToken&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;cancellationToken&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Now, Orders only depends on the contract:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight csharp"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;using&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nn"&gt;Catalog.Contracts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;namespace&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nn"&gt;Orders.Application&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;public&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;sealed&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;class&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;CreateOrderHandler&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;IProductReader&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;products&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// ...&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Catalog can implement this interface internally.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Orders doesn't need to know whether the data comes from Entity Framework Core, an HTTP service, or something else.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We've created an explicit module boundary.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Architecture Guardian provides two complementary checks for this scenario:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;AG001&lt;/strong&gt; validates whether a dependency between modules is allowed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;AG004&lt;/strong&gt; detects cross-module access to implementation projects instead of declared public contracts.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One important detail: a class being marked &lt;code&gt;public&lt;/code&gt; in C# doesn't automatically make it a public architectural contract.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That distinction is particularly important in modular monoliths.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  When the problem is bigger than one project
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Now let's look at another interesting case.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Imagine we have two modules, Orders and Billing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Orders needs some information from Billing, and Billing also needs some information from Orders.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Each module exposes its own Contracts project.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The dependencies look like this:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;Orders.Application
    depends on Billing.Contracts

Billing.Application
    depends on Orders.Contracts
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;There is no circular project reference here.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The solution can build successfully because the contract projects don't reference each other.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;However, at the logical module level, we have created a cycle.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Orders depends on Billing, and Billing depends on Orders.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Is this always a catastrophic problem?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Not necessarily. But if our architectural goal is to keep modules independent and prevent cycles, we need to detect it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is what &lt;strong&gt;AG003&lt;/strong&gt; is designed for.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It identifies cycles between logical modules using a complete solution analysis.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And this brings us to an important distinction between Roslyn analyzers and solution-level architecture checks.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Why Roslyn analyzers are not enough
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Roslyn analyzers are extremely useful because they give us feedback directly while coding.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Architecture Guardian includes a Roslyn analyzer that can detect supported architectural violations within the compilation being analyzed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is valuable for developers because they don't have to run a separate command every time they modify a dependency.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But a Roslyn analyzer doesn't have the complete view of every relationship in a solution.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, the logical module cycle we just discussed requires examining dependencies across multiple projects.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's why Architecture Guardian also provides a solution scanner.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The responsibilities are different:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Roslyn analyzer:&lt;/strong&gt; immediate feedback for supported rules within the current compilation.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;CLI:&lt;/strong&gt; full solution analysis, including logical module cycles, with a gate suitable for automation.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;MCP server:&lt;/strong&gt; architectural context and analysis tools for AI coding agents.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The analyzer currently covers AG001, AG002, and AG004 through AG006 within its compilation scope.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AG003 requires the solution scan.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So, having no diagnostics in the editor is useful, but it doesn't mean the entire solution is architecturally compliant.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Enforcing the rules in CI
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Now that we have defined our architecture, there's one more thing we should do.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Make sure that no change can be merged without validating it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Since Architecture Guardian provides a CLI, we can execute the scan in an Azure DevOps pipeline, GitHub Actions workflow, or another CI environment.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight yaml"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;steps&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="pi"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;script&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="pi"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="s"&gt;dotnet restore Shop.slnx \&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="s"&gt;-p:Configuration=Release&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;displayName&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;Restore solution&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="pi"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;script&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="pi"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="s"&gt;archguard scan Shop.slnx \&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="s"&gt;--policy architecture.guardian.yaml \&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="s"&gt;--configuration Release \&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="s"&gt;--trusted&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;displayName&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;Validate architecture&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;This example assumes the agent already has the CLI and a compatible .NET SDK installed, and that the repository is trusted.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The scanner returns different exit codes depending on the result.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A successful scan passes the gate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An architectural violation can fail the pipeline.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And if analysis cannot establish compliance, that is reported separately rather than being considered a successful architecture check.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This distinction is important.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A tool that couldn't analyze your solution shouldn't tell you that your architecture is valid.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For existing applications, Architecture Guardian also supports baselines and change-oriented analysis, which can help teams introduce enforcement progressively instead of fixing every historical violation immediately.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  And what about AI coding agents?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is probably one of the most interesting parts of the project for me.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We're increasingly using AI coding agents to implement features, refactor code, and work across entire repositories.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;They can generate quite impressive code.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But they can also introduce architectural violations.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Let's return to our modular monolith.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Imagine giving an agent the following task:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;"Implement a feature in Orders that retrieves product information from Catalog."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The agent explores the codebase and discovers &lt;code&gt;Catalog.Infrastructure.ProductRepository&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The class is public.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The method already does what the agent needs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So, it adds the reference and implements the feature.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The result can be functional.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But the agent has completely bypassed the architectural contract we wanted to preserve.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Was the agent wrong?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;From a functional perspective, perhaps not.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;From an architectural perspective, yes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The problem is that the agent may not know our architectural decisions, especially when those decisions are only documented in a README or an architecture diagram.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Giving agents architectural context
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Architecture Guardian provides an MCP server that exposes architectural information to compatible AI coding agents.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Instead of simply asking an agent to respect Clean Architecture, we can give it tools to inspect the actual architectural policy.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The MCP integration exposes operations such as:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;get_architecture&lt;/code&gt; to discover modules, layers, and dependency permissions.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;list_rules&lt;/code&gt; to understand available rules.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;analyze_solution&lt;/code&gt; to validate changes against the solution.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;explain_violation&lt;/code&gt; to understand a reported problem and its possible remediation.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;analyze_changes&lt;/code&gt; to inspect findings relative to Git changes.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Let's imagine a different workflow.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Before implementing the feature, the agent asks Architecture Guardian for the solution's architectural context.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It discovers that Orders is allowed to access Catalog through its contract project, but not through Infrastructure.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The agent implements the feature using &lt;code&gt;IProductReader&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then it runs the architecture analysis.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If a violation is reported, it can inspect the diagnostic and correct its implementation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The difference is that the agent no longer has to rely only on natural-language instructions to understand our architectural boundaries.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It can query them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Of course, MCP doesn't guarantee that an agent will always make the right architectural decision.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And it doesn't replace the CI gate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But it gives agents access to the same architecture policy that developers and pipelines use.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is the direction I wanted to explore with Architecture Guardian.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Does this replace architecture tests or code reviews?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No, and I think it's important to clarify this.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We already have libraries and approaches for writing architecture tests in .NET.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;They're useful, and Architecture Guardian isn't intended to make them irrelevant.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The goal is to make architectural rules available throughout the development workflow, instead of limiting validation to tests that someone must remember to run.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It also doesn't replace code reviews.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An architectural rule can verify that Application doesn't depend on Infrastructure.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But it cannot decide whether your abstraction is well designed, whether your aggregates make sense, or whether you've selected the appropriate architecture for your business requirements.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Those decisions still require engineering judgment.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The tool is there to enforce the boundaries we've explicitly declared.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And we should be careful not to create unnecessary restrictions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The goal is to protect our architecture, not to make development more complicated.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Let's try it
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Architecture Guardian is available on GitHub and distributed through NuGet.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You can start by installing the CLI:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;dotnet tool &lt;span class="nb"&gt;install&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--global&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="se"&gt;\&lt;/span&gt;
  ArchitectureGuardian.Cli &lt;span class="se"&gt;\&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--version&lt;/span&gt; 0.3.0
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Create your &lt;code&gt;architecture.guardian.yaml&lt;/code&gt; policy, restore the solution, and run the scanner.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There is also a runnable Clean Architecture example in the documentation, containing both compliant and intentionally violating configurations.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You can experiment with different dependency violations and observe the diagnostics without modifying your own application.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you want to integrate the checks into your IDE, the Roslyn analyzer package is also available.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And if you're experimenting with AI coding agents, you can explore the MCP integration.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The full setup instructions and examples are available here:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Documentation:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://kbourzayq.github.io/arch-guard/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://kbourzayq.github.io/arch-guard/&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;GitHub:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://github.com/kbourzayq/arch-guard" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://github.com/kbourzayq/arch-guard&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Final Thoughts
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To conclude, I think we sometimes spend a lot of effort designing software architectures without putting the same effort into preserving them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We discuss dependency rules, module boundaries, separation of concerns, and all the principles that should help us build maintainable software.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But as the application grows, it's surprisingly easy to introduce dependencies that contradict those decisions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And most of the time, the compiler won't complain.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My intention with Architecture Guardian is not to introduce another architectural framework or tell developers how they should design their applications.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It's to provide a way to &lt;strong&gt;define architectural decisions once and continuously verify them&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A last piece of advice: don't try to enforce every possible architectural rule from the first day, especially if you're working on a large existing application.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Start with the boundaries that matter most, understand your existing dependencies, and progressively introduce stricter validation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Otherwise, the tool intended to protect your architecture might become an unnecessary obstacle for your team.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's it!&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Architecture Guardian is open source, and feedback is more than welcome.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'm particularly interested in hearing how other teams manage architectural boundaries in large .NET solutions, modular monoliths, and AI-assisted development workflows.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Feel free to try it, challenge the approach, open an issue, or contribute.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;See you soon!&lt;/p&gt;

