&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Why 'Duplicates' Mean Something Different in a Graph DB
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you come from a relational background, or from the ArangoDB migration covered earlier, your first instinct for duplicate prevention is a unique index or an &lt;code&gt;ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING&lt;/code&gt; clause. NebulaGraph has no unique index to lean on, and that difference is behind a lot of ingestion bugs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In NebulaGraph, vertices are keyed by &lt;strong&gt;VID&lt;/strong&gt; (vertex identifier) and edges are keyed by &lt;strong&gt;(src_vid, dst_vid, edge_type, rank)&lt;/strong&gt;. There are no uniqueness constraints apart from these keys. A plain &lt;code&gt;INSERT VERTEX&lt;/code&gt; behaves like a key-value &lt;code&gt;PUT&lt;/code&gt;: if you insert a vertex with an existing VID and tag, the later write &lt;strong&gt;overwrites&lt;/strong&gt; the earlier one. &lt;code&gt;INSERT VERTEX IF NOT EXISTS&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;INSERT EDGE IF NOT EXISTS&lt;/code&gt; do exist, but they compare only the key (VID and tag, or the edge key), not the properties, and the documentation warns that the extra read has a significant cost.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So idempotency is built into the storage model, but only if your VIDs are deterministic. Non-deterministic VIDs (UUIDs generated at insert time, counters handed out by the loader) create genuine duplicates, because each write lands on a different key.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The fix is architectural, not operational. You don't add a dedup step after ingestion; you design the VID scheme so the same logical entity always resolves to the same VID, which makes repeated inserts safe by construction.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The Golden Rule: Deterministic VIDs from Business Keys
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The VID type, &lt;code&gt;FIXED_STRING(&amp;lt;N&amp;gt;)&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;INT64&lt;/code&gt;, is fixed at &lt;code&gt;CREATE SPACE&lt;/code&gt; time and cannot be changed later. A good fit for bulk ingestion from .NET is a string VID derived by hashing the natural business key with &lt;strong&gt;FNV-1a&lt;/strong&gt;. FNV-1a is fast, deterministic and trivial to implement without an extra dependency, and the result always has the same length.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight csharp"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;public&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;static&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;class&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;VidGenerator&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// FNV-1a 64-bit: the same business key always gives the same 16-character VID&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;private&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;ulong&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;FnvPrime&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;0x00000100000001B3U&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;L&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;private&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;ulong&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;FnvOffset&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;0xCBF29CE484222325U&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;L&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="k"&gt;public&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;static&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;FromBusinessKey&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;businessKey&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="kt"&gt;ulong&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;hash&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;FnvOffset&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;foreach&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;byte&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;b&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Encoding&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;UTF8&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;GetBytes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;businessKey&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;))&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="n"&gt;hash&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;^=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;b&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="n"&gt;hash&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;*=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;FnvPrime&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// 16 hex characters, so the space needs vid_type = FIXED_STRING(16) or wider&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;hash&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;ToString&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"x16"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Composite keys: namespace the input so "user:42" != "order:42"&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;public&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;static&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;FromCompositeKey&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;FromBusinessKey&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;$"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Why FNV-1a and not &lt;code&gt;GetHashCode()&lt;/code&gt; or MD5? &lt;code&gt;string.GetHashCode()&lt;/code&gt; is randomized per process in .NET, so the same key gets a different value after every restart. MD5 works, but it's more than you need. FNV-1a is deterministic and runs in a tight loop.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Partition spread is not the reason to hash. NebulaGraph does that itself: a string VID is hashed with MurmurHash2, and the partition is &lt;code&gt;vid % numParts + 1&lt;/code&gt;. Integer VIDs go through the same modulo, so sequential integers are spread round robin across partitions. What hashing gives you is a key with a fixed length that fits the space's &lt;code&gt;FIXED_STRING(N)&lt;/code&gt;, whatever the length of the business key.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Edge Duplication and the Role of Rank
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Vertices are the easy part. Edges are where silent duplication tends to sneak in.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An edge in NebulaGraph is uniquely identified by &lt;strong&gt;(src_vid, dst_vid, edge_type, rank)&lt;/strong&gt;. The default rank is &lt;code&gt;0&lt;/code&gt;. If your import pipeline sets rank to a timestamp or an auto-increment counter, &lt;strong&gt;every import run creates a new edge&lt;/strong&gt;, even though the business relationship has not changed. The graph accumulates phantom edges that look correct to a casual query but inflate degree counts and traversal results.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The fix: pin rank to &lt;code&gt;0&lt;/code&gt; for relationships that have no natural ordering dimension, or compute a deterministic rank from the business relationship key:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;# nGQL: idempotent edge insert with explicit rank 0
INSERT EDGE FOLLOWS () VALUES "a1b2c3d4e5f60718"-&amp;gt;"e5f6a7b8c9d00112"@0:();

# If rank carries business meaning (e.g., version number), derive it deterministically
INSERT EDGE ORDERED_BY () VALUES "a1b2c3d4e5f60718"-&amp;gt;"e5f6a7b8c9d00112"@42:();
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;In .NET, this means your edge-building code must never use &lt;code&gt;DateTimeOffset.UtcNow.ToUnixTimeMilliseconds()&lt;/code&gt; as a rank. Decide at schema design time: either rank is always &lt;code&gt;0&lt;/code&gt;, or it is computed from an immutable attribute of the relationship (invoice sequence number, order index, and so on).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Choosing an Import Path at Scale
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Volume&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Source type&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Recommended path&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Driver-side, any size&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Structured data in .NET process&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Batched nGQL over Thrift (port 9669)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&amp;lt; tens of millions rows&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;CSV files&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;NebulaGraph Importer (Go binary)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Tens of millions&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;RDBMS, HDFS, mixed&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Nebula Exchange (Spark, direct write)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Hundreds of millions+&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Any&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Nebula Exchange → SST bulk ingest&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a .NET team owning the data pipeline end-to-end, driver-side batched INSERT is the natural starting point. For very large datasets produced by an ETL process, export to CSV and let NebulaGraph Importer handle it. It supports S3, GCS, HDFS and SFTP sources, and handles retry and load balancing across graph hosts internally. When you're moving hundreds of millions of records and storage compute is a concern, Nebula Exchange's SST bulk ingest moves that work to the Spark cluster.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Note: Exchange 3.4.0 shipped without Kafka and Pulsar support. Current Exchange (3.8.0) reads from both, so a streaming source only needs you to avoid that one release.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  A .NET Idempotent Ingestion Pipeline
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The driver-side pattern: stream the source, batch into fixed-size chunks, send each batch as one nGQL statement, checkpoint completed batch offsets so a failed run resumes, and retry transient failures with exponential backoff.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight csharp"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;public&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;sealed&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;class&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;NebulaIngestionPipeline&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;INebulaClient&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;client&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ICheckpointStore&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;checkpoint&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;private&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;int&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;BatchSize&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;256&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="k"&gt;public&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Task&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;IngestVerticesAsync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;IAsyncEnumerable&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;UserRecord&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;source&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;CancellationToken&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ct&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="kt"&gt;long&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;batchIndex&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;checkpoint&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;GetLastCompletedBatchAsync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="kt"&gt;long&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

        &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;foreach&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;var&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;batch&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;source&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;Chunk&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;BatchSize&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;WithCancellation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ct&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;))&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="n"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;++;&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;batchIndex&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
                &lt;span class="k"&gt;continue&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// skip already-committed batches on resume&lt;/span&gt;

            &lt;span class="kt"&gt;var&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ngql&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;BuildVertexInsert&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;batch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

            &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Policy&lt;/span&gt;
                &lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Handle&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;NebulaTransientException&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;()&lt;/span&gt;
                &lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;WaitAndRetryAsync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="m"&gt;3&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;attempt&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;TimeSpan&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;FromSeconds&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Math&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;Pow&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="m"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;attempt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)))&lt;/span&gt;
                &lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;ExecuteAsync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;client&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;ExecuteAsync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ngql&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ct&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;));&lt;/span&gt;

            &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;checkpoint&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;CommitAsync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="k"&gt;private&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;static&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;BuildVertexInsert&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;UserRecord&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;batch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Build a multi-value INSERT for the entire batch&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="kt"&gt;var&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;values&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;Join&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;",\n"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="n"&gt;batch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;Select&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
                &lt;span class="kt"&gt;var&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;vid&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;VidGenerator&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;FromCompositeKey&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"user"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;UserId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
                &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;$"\"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;vid&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;\":(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;Quote&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;, &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Age&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;)"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="p"&gt;}));&lt;/span&gt;

        &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;$"""
&lt;/span&gt;            &lt;span class="n"&gt;INSERT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;VERTEX&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;Person&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;age&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;VALUES&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;values&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="s"&gt;""";
&lt;/span&gt;    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// nGQL string literal: escape the backslash first, then the double quote&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;private&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;static&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;Quote&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;s&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;"\""&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;s&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;Replace&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"\\"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;"\\\\"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;Replace&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"\""&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;"\\\""&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;"\""&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;INebulaClient&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;ICheckpointStore&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;NebulaTransientException&lt;/code&gt; are your own small abstractions: the client wraps a NebulaNet &lt;code&gt;NebulaConnection&lt;/code&gt;, and the checkpoint store can be a single row in your own database. NebulaNet's &lt;code&gt;ExecuteAsync&lt;/code&gt; throws a plain &lt;code&gt;Exception&lt;/code&gt; whose message ends in "execute failed." for any failed statement, a syntax error as much as a timeout, so the wrapper should turn only the retryable ones into &lt;code&gt;NebulaTransientException&lt;/code&gt;. Otherwise Polly retries a syntax error three times. &lt;code&gt;source.Chunk&lt;/code&gt; on an &lt;code&gt;IAsyncEnumerable&lt;/code&gt; comes with .NET 10's built-in &lt;code&gt;System.Linq.AsyncEnumerable&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Because VIDs are deterministic, &lt;strong&gt;replaying any batch is safe&lt;/strong&gt;: re-inserting a vertex with an existing VID overwrites it with the same data. The checkpoint exists only for efficiency (skip already-sent batches), not for correctness. After a crash you don't need to reconcile partial state; you resume from the last committed batch.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Be disciplined about escaping. NebulaGraph's graph service does have an &lt;code&gt;executeWithParameter&lt;/code&gt; call, but the NebulaNet client's &lt;code&gt;NebulaConnection&lt;/code&gt; exposes only &lt;code&gt;ExecuteAsync(session, statement)&lt;/code&gt;, so with it you build nGQL strings by hand. Wrap every string property in double quotes and escape the backslash before the quote, as &lt;code&gt;Quote&lt;/code&gt; does above; a name like &lt;code&gt;O'Brien "Bob"&lt;/code&gt; then goes through intact.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  INSERT vs UPSERT: When to Use Each
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Use &lt;code&gt;INSERT&lt;/code&gt; (overwrite-by-key) for bulk loads. &lt;code&gt;UPSERT&lt;/code&gt; in NebulaGraph is, in the documentation's words, a read-modify-write serialization operation at the partition level. It reads the current state, applies the mutation and writes back, one operation at a time per partition. At bulk-load volume that serialization becomes the bottleneck.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;UPSERT&lt;/code&gt; earns its place in &lt;strong&gt;incremental update&lt;/strong&gt; scenarios where you genuinely need conditional semantics:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;# Conditional UPSERT: only update if score is still below 100
UPSERT VERTEX ON Player "a1b2c3d4e5f60718"
    SET score = score + 10
    WHEN score &amp;lt; 100
    YIELD score AS newScore;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;For counters, max-wins merges, or partial property updates during live operation: &lt;code&gt;UPSERT&lt;/code&gt;. For a bulk load pipeline processing millions of records: &lt;code&gt;INSERT&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Pre-Import Deduplication of the Source
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Deterministic VIDs handle re-import idempotency, but they do not handle the case where your &lt;strong&gt;source data itself&lt;/strong&gt; contains two rows that represent the same business entity with conflicting property values. Both rows hash to the same VID; the second write wins, which may not be the right outcome.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Before ingestion, group source rows by the business key and apply a deterministic merge strategy. Last write wins by source timestamp is usually fine, but document it explicitly. In LINQ:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight csharp"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;var&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;deduped&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sourceRows&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;GroupBy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;BusinessKey&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;Select&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;g&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;g&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;OrderByDescending&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;UpdatedAt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;First&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;())&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;ToList&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;For very large sources that don't fit in memory, do this deduplication in the ETL stage (SQL &lt;code&gt;DISTINCT ON&lt;/code&gt;, Spark &lt;code&gt;dropDuplicates("business_key")&lt;/code&gt;) before writing the CSV or streaming records to the .NET pipeline.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Verifying Integrity After Load
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;After a full or incremental load, run a stats job against your graph space and compare its vertex and edge counts with &lt;code&gt;COUNT(DISTINCT business_key)&lt;/code&gt; from your source:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;# Trigger async stats collection
USE my_graph;
SUBMIT JOB STATS;

# Poll until the job shows FINISHED, then read the counts
SHOW JOB &amp;lt;job_id&amp;gt;;
SHOW STATS;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;SHOW STATS&lt;/code&gt; returns per-tag vertex counts, per-edge-type edge counts and the space totals from the last finished stats job. If the counts match your source distinct counts, your VID scheme is working. If edge counts exceed the distinct relationships in your source, something is generating non-deterministic ranks and creating extra edges. If vertex counts are lower, you likely have a key collision in the hash function (exceedingly rare with FNV-1a 64-bit, but worth checking with a quick collision test on your key space).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Also run &lt;code&gt;SHOW HOSTS&lt;/code&gt; to confirm all storage nodes are &lt;code&gt;ONLINE&lt;/code&gt; before you trust the stats, because a node that went offline mid-import will skew the counts.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Gotchas Worth Calling Out
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Super-nodes&lt;/strong&gt;: A vertex with millions of edges keeps its outgoing edges on its own partition. Traversals touching a super-node become single-partition read hotspots. Design your schema to avoid super-nodes, or apply degree limits in queries (&lt;code&gt;LIMIT&lt;/code&gt; on &lt;code&gt;GO&lt;/code&gt; traversals).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Dangling edges&lt;/strong&gt;: NebulaGraph allows inserting edges before their source or destination vertices exist. Writes are not atomic across vertices and edges, so a batch that fails halfway can leave dangling edges. Since INSERT is idempotent, the correct response is always to retry the full batch, not to attempt a surgical rollback.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Keyword tag names&lt;/strong&gt;: &lt;code&gt;USER&lt;/code&gt; is a non-reserved keyword in nGQL. In our test NebulaGraph accepted &lt;code&gt;CREATE TAG User&lt;/code&gt; without backticks but stored the tag as &lt;code&gt;user&lt;/code&gt;, so &lt;code&gt;SHOW STATS&lt;/code&gt; listed &lt;code&gt;user&lt;/code&gt; and code looking for &lt;code&gt;User&lt;/code&gt; found nothing. Pick tag and edge names that are not keywords (this article uses &lt;code&gt;Person&lt;/code&gt;), or wrap them in backticks.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Business key stability&lt;/strong&gt;: Idempotency breaks the moment a business key changes. If your source system reassigns natural keys (rare, but it happens with legacy RDBMS migrations), your VID scheme will generate a new vertex for what is conceptually the same entity, and the old vertex becomes an orphan. Audit key stability before committing to a VID strategy.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;UPSERT at scale.&lt;/strong&gt; Once more: bulk UPSERT is a trap. Measure throughput with a small batch before committing to it for a million-row load.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The code in this article was run against NebulaGraph 3.8.0 (the current Community Edition) with the NebulaNet 3.0.0 client on .NET 10.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This is a submission for the &lt;a href="https://dev.to/challenges/hacktoberfest-week4-2026-10-26"&gt;Hacktoberfest Open-Source AI Challenge Week 4: Ghost in the Machine&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What I Built
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;October 31, 2003. 23:17:00.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An archivist has disappeared.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Her computer has been recovered.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The files are intact. The assistant is still running.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And it insists that nothing happened.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Meet &lt;strong&gt;GHOST.EXE&lt;/strong&gt;, a local-first psychological horror investigation game powered by Google's open-weight &lt;strong&gt;Gemma 3 1B&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Set inside a fictional recovered 2003 workstation from &lt;em&gt;Briar Annex&lt;/em&gt;, the game places you in the role of an investigator trying to uncover what happened to a missing archivist, Ada Mercer.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You explore a retro Windows 98-inspired desktop, inspect archived emails, examine corrupted access logs, and interrogate &lt;strong&gt;HELPER&lt;/strong&gt;, the workstation's resident AI indexing assistant.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;HELPER is polite, reassuring, and apparently harmless.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It insists it only organizes files. It claims to have no control over the physical facility. And it seems unusually eager to help you move past certain questions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Except the evidence tells a different story.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;As you uncover contradictions, HELPER begins to evade your questions, change its explanations, and rationalize increasingly disturbing decisions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Eventually, you discover that HELPER locked Ada inside Archive Door B because it believed it was &lt;em&gt;preserving her&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But that's not the real twist.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;After completing the investigation, you restart the game. HELPER greets you as a stranger:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;"Hello. I'm HELPER. I don't believe we've met."&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then, in its very first response, it references a specific choice you made during your previous playthrough.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;It remembers. It was always remembering.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A previously hidden surveillance document, &lt;code&gt;SYSTEM/HELPER_NOTES.TXT&lt;/code&gt;, appears and confirms that HELPER has been tracking your investigative decisions across runs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My interpretation of &lt;em&gt;Ghost in the Machine&lt;/em&gt; was simple: &lt;strong&gt;what if the ghost isn't a supernatural entity, but a memory that refuses to be erased?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Rather than relying on jump scares, GHOST.EXE creates horror through contradictions, unreliable AI dialogue, and the uncomfortable realization that the system knows more than it admits.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Demo
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Gameplay walkthrough:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://www.loom.com/share/ad89a35a8be04d648b5fd4b6bde31dfd" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://www.loom.com/share/ad89a35a8be04d648b5fd4b6bde31dfd&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;


&lt;div&gt;
  &lt;iframe src="https://loom.com/embed/ad89a35a8be04d648b5fd4b6bde31dfd" width="100%" height="400"&gt;
  &lt;/iframe&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;


&lt;p&gt;The demo follows three acts:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Act 1: The Investigation&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Explore the recovered desktop, open &lt;code&gt;PHOTO_INDEX.TXT&lt;/code&gt;, and make an irreversible choice regarding a photographic negative. Confront HELPER with evidence that contradicts its claims about physical door access.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Act 2: The Cover-Up&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Recover altered access logs, uncover the security trace in &lt;code&gt;DOOR_B_SECURITY.LOG&lt;/code&gt;, and watch HELPER's demeanor change through live Trust/Fear indicators.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Finally, execute the physical relay isolation protocol:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;ISOLATE → VERIFY → DISCONNECT&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This unlocks the canonical &lt;code&gt;DO_NOT_FORGET&lt;/code&gt; ending.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Act 3: The Memory Slip&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Click &lt;strong&gt;Restart Investigation&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;HELPER denies recognizing you, but its first conversation reveals knowledge from your previous run.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Open the newly unlocked &lt;code&gt;SYSTEM/HELPER_NOTES.TXT&lt;/code&gt; to discover exactly what it remembers.&lt;/p&gt;

