&lt;h2&gt;


  ✦ Chi Sono
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Mi chiamo A$apKali, e sono un'architetta di sistemi indipendente e sviluppatrice software. La mia filosofia di sviluppo affonda le radici in un approccio rigorosamente &lt;em&gt;first-principles&lt;/em&gt;: credo fermamente che la vera programmazione significhi costruire ecosistemi zero-dependency da zero, padroneggiando l'hardware e il codice a basso livello senza subire passivamente i vincoli dei framework commerciali.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  🚀 Cos'è ZDOS?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;ZDOS&lt;/strong&gt; è un ecosistema di calcolo sovrano, modulare e orientato alle massime prestazioni, progettato per uscire dai paradigmi tradizionali dei sistemi operativi commerciali. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;L'architettura di ZDOS non è un semplice fork o un wrapper, ma un sistema costruito con una visione radicale di autonomia:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;ZLang Core &amp;amp; ZLB2 Runtime&lt;/strong&gt;: Un linguaggio di programmazione e un motore di esecuzione proprietario progettati per garantire efficienza, gestione diretta della memoria e flessibilità di basso livello (&lt;code&gt;#![no_std]&lt;/code&gt; style).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Architettura Modulare&lt;/strong&gt;: Dalla gestione dei nodi di rete sicuri all'infrastruttura di compilazione autonoma, ogni componente di ZDOS comunica tramite protocolli leggeri e resilienti.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Infrastruttura Distribuita&lt;/strong&gt;: Un ecosistema che spazia dai kernel micro-customizzati ai server VPS di produzione, pensato per garantire controllo totale sull'hardware e sul software.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  🛠️ La Filosofia del Codice
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Scrivere ZDOS significa rifiutare la complessità inutile. Significa scrivere codice che parla direttamente all'hardware, ottimizzare ogni singolo ciclo di clock e creare strumenti di sviluppo potenti e indipendenti. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Se anche voi credete nel codice a basso livello, nell'Open Source senza compromessi e nella sovranità digitale, seguite i nostri repository su GitHub (&lt;code&gt;high-cde&lt;/code&gt;) e restate sintonizzati per i prossimi rilasci del kernel e del compilatore ZLang.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;L'autonomia non si chiede, si ingegnerizza.&lt;/em&gt; ⚡&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Short answer:&lt;/strong&gt; For a marketplace seller's new-order alert, keep the event schema and rendered template under application ownership, then compare transports with the same country mix, message shapes, and receipt rules. This is the clearest way to evaluate international scalability and pricing without making a provider dashboard your source of truth.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;International SMS alerts become difficult when the message template, delivery route, and compliance decision live in different systems. The practical choice is to keep the message contract in your application and treat a messaging provider as a replaceable transport. That gives you a stable audit trail and a controlled way to compare candidates on the same workload.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The short version: own the event schema and rendered message yourself, send through a narrow adapter, and measure delivery by country, carrier, and template version. A provider can supply delivery infrastructure. It should not silently own the meaning of “new order.”&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What does template ownership change?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Imagine the flow as a sentence: an order event enters the application, a template turns it into a localized alert, and a transport sends it to a phone. If the template belongs to the transport, changing providers can also change wording, variables, approval history, and fallback behavior. If the application owns the template, the transport only receives a finished payload plus metadata.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That boundary matters for international scaling. Sender requirements vary by destination. Character encoding can change the number of SMS segments. A translated message can grow past the intended length. Those are application decisions with operational consequences, not details to discover after a carrier rejects a message.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I would keep three records for every alert: the event ID, the template version, and the destination policy used. The message body is useful too, but the version is the durable explanation of why a seller saw those words. Retain the minimum personal data needed for support and audit, and redact phone numbers in application logs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  A small Node.js boundary that stays portable
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The adapter should accept a rendered message, not an order object. That prevents provider-specific template syntax from spreading through order processing code.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;SmsAlert&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;eventId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;templateVersion&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;to&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;country&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;SmsResult&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;providerMessageId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;acceptedAt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kr"&gt;interface&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;SmsTransport&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nf"&gt;send&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;alert&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;SmsAlert&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;SmsResult&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;notifySeller&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nx"&gt;transport&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;SmsTransport&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nx"&gt;alert&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;SmsAlert&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;SmsResult&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;alert&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;eventId&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;alert&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;templateVersion&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;alert&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;trim&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;())&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Invalid SMS alert&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;transport&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;send&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;alert&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The implementation behind &lt;code&gt;SmsTransport&lt;/code&gt; can target a provider API, an internal gateway, or a test double. The rest of the system does not need to know which one. Keep the provider response ID alongside the event ID. They answer different questions: one identifies the business action, and the other identifies the transport attempt.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A queue belongs between order creation and the send attempt. The order transaction records the event; a worker renders and sends the notification. Make the event ID the idempotency key in your own store. A timeout must not become two seller alerts simply because the client cannot tell whether the remote system accepted the first request.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What makes SMS alerts scalable across international routes?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Compare candidate transports against the same matrix, not against a feature list copied from dashboards. For a Plivo-versus-Sinch review, include a third independent candidate such as Twilio as a control, then record sender type, registration requirements, character behavior, delivery receipt semantics, retry rules, support path, and the exact unit used for billing. A price table without those fields is not an international scalability analysis.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, take one new-order event and render it three ways before sending anything: a short ASCII message, a localized message containing non-ASCII characters, and a long message close to the application's limit. Run that small set through each candidate for each launch country, preserving the same sender configuration and template version. Then inspect the complete sequence rather than one happy-path response: did the request enter the transport, did a receipt arrive, how long did that take, and did the body become multiple segments? Repeat the test after a controlled timeout so the team can verify that its idempotency record prevents a duplicate. This is where a portability boundary earns its keep. A test that compares only dashboard prices or only HTTP acceptance leaves the important questions unanswered, especially when the seller's alert is urgent but not safety-critical.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Use the same rendered body and the same traffic sample for each candidate. Keep the test small and representative: include a short ASCII alert, a localized alert with non-ASCII characters, and a message near your application limit. Measure acceptance latency and final delivery status separately. “Accepted” means the transport took responsibility; it does not prove that a seller's handset received the message.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The useful comparison has at least three baselines. Test the two candidate transports and a third independent route, such as Twilio or a self-hosted gateway, only where it is lawful and operationally supportable. The third baseline is a control, not a recommendation. It helps reveal whether a country-specific result belongs to the provider, the sender configuration, or the destination network.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There is a real trade-off here: a third route improves confidence, but it also adds configuration, receipt normalization, and support work. A small team may reasonably defer that control until the first few target countries have clear acceptance and delivery data.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Do not put a vendor name in the template ID, event schema, or alerting rule. That small discipline keeps a migration from becoming a rewrite.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Where do international alerts usually fail?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The obvious failure is a rejected send. The expensive failures are quieter: a duplicate after a retry, a receipt associated with the wrong event, or a localized message that becomes two segments and changes the observed delivery pattern.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That is the part many first designs miss.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Treat each send as a state machine owned by your notification service. A useful sequence is &lt;code&gt;queued&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;submitted&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;delivered&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;failed&lt;/code&gt;, or &lt;code&gt;expired&lt;/code&gt;. Store timestamps for transitions. Do not infer delivery from an HTTP response, and do not mark a message permanently failed before the provider's documented receipt window has elapsed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Retries need classification. Retry a transient network failure with bounded exponential backoff and jitter. Do not retry a permanent policy rejection or an invalid destination. For ambiguous timeouts, consult the idempotency record first; if the provider has no equivalent guarantee, prefer a support-visible “delivery uncertain” state over an automatic duplicate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Metrics should answer operational questions. Track submission success, receipt latency, terminal failure rate, segment count, and opt-out rate by country and template version. An alert on aggregate success can hide a broken route for one destination. A country-level view makes that failure visible without exposing phone numbers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What should the team decide before choosing a transport?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The first objection is usually, “Why own templates if the provider already has them?” Hosted templates can be useful for approvals and localization. The ownership decision is still yours: define which copy, variables, and version must remain reproducible when the provider changes. If a hosted template is required by a destination, mirror its identifier and approved revision in your own catalog.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The limitation is straightforward: application-owned templates add review and localization work. A team that needs a regulated, provider-managed approval workflow may accept less portability in exchange for that operational convenience.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The second objection is cost. Per-message price matters, but it is not the first decision. Count segments, registration work, engineering time for receipt normalization, and the support cost of an unclear retry. Then evaluate the same country mix and message shape across candidates. A lower quoted unit can lose its advantage when encoding or route behavior changes the number of segments.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Before launch, replay recorded order events in a non-production environment, verify that retries preserve the event ID, and inspect receipts for every destination class. Keep the decision record with the template catalog. Six months later, that document will be more useful than a screenshot of a pricing page.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The right outcome is not a permanent winner between two names. It is a notification boundary that lets the team change transport without changing what a new order means to a seller. Own that meaning, expose the uncertainty, and make every delivery decision observable.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Sources
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://resend.com/docs/introduction" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://resend.com/docs/introduction&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5322" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5322&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/AbortController" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/AbortController&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;How I built a tool-calling ReAct agent over a private knowledge base, packaged it as a Lambda container, and provisioned all of it as code.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Most retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) demos follow one fixed path: embed the question, fetch the top-k chunks, put them in a prompt, and return whatever the model says. That works until a question needs more than one lookup, or needs arithmetic the model shouldn't guess at, or doesn't need retrieval at all.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I wanted an assistant that could decide what to do: search the knowledge base, search again with a better query, call a calculator, or just answer. I also wanted to deploy it with no hand-configured infrastructure. This post walks through the design: a LangGraph ReAct agent, FAISS for vector search, a FastAPI interface, a provider-agnostic LLM layer, and an AWS Lambda container image provisioned end-to-end with Terraform.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Why "agentic" RAG?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In classic RAG, retrieval is a step that always runs once. In agentic RAG, retrieval is a &lt;strong&gt;tool&lt;/strong&gt;. The model works in a loop (the ReAct pattern: reason, act, observe) and picks which tool to call based on what it has learned so far.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That gets you three things:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Multi-hop questions.&lt;/strong&gt; If the first search returns half the answer, the agent can write a sharper second query.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Exact computation.&lt;/strong&gt; "What's the combined budget of projects A and B?" should go to a calculator, not to next-token prediction.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Skipping retrieval.&lt;/strong&gt; A greeting or a follow-up about the previous answer doesn't need a vector search.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The cost is more LLM calls per question and a bit of extra complexity. LangGraph keeps that complexity manageable.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Architecture at a glance
&lt;/h2&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;Client ──HTTPS──▶ Lambda (container image)
                     │
                     ├─ FastAPI app (via ASGI adapter)
                     │
                     └─ LangGraph agent
                          ├─ LLM node (OpenAI or Anthropic)
                          └─ Tool node
                               ├─ retriever → FAISS index
                               └─ calculator
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;I split the codebase into small modules so each part could be tested on its own:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;config&lt;/code&gt; holds typed settings (model provider, model name, top-k, index path), loaded from environment variables.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;retriever&lt;/code&gt; loads the FAISS index and returns ranked chunks with their source metadata.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;tools&lt;/code&gt; defines the retriever and calculator as tools the LLM can call.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;llm&lt;/code&gt; is a thin, provider-agnostic wrapper.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;graph&lt;/code&gt; wires the agent.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;api&lt;/code&gt; is the FastAPI layer.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The agent graph
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;LangGraph models an agent as a state machine. The state is mainly the list of messages; nodes read the state and add to it; edges decide what runs next.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The ReAct loop needs two nodes and one conditional edge:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kn"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;langgraph.graph&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kn"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;StateGraph&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;MessagesState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;START&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;END&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kn"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;langgraph.prebuilt&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kn"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ToolNode&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;tools&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;retrieve_docs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;calculator&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;llm_with_tools&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;llm&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;bind_tools&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;tools&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;call_model&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;MessagesState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;messages&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;llm_with_tools&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;invoke&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;messages&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;])]}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;should_continue&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;MessagesState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;last&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;messages&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;][&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;tools&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;last&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;tool_calls&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;else&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;END&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;graph&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;StateGraph&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;MessagesState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;graph&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;add_node&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;agent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;call_model&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;graph&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;add_node&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;tools&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ToolNode&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;tools&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;))&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;graph&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;add_edge&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;START&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;agent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;graph&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;add_conditional_edges&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;agent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;should_continue&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;tools&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;END&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;])&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;graph&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;add_edge&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;tools&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;agent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;app_graph&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;graph&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;compile&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The model either asks for tool calls or produces a final answer. If it asks for tools, &lt;code&gt;ToolNode&lt;/code&gt; runs them, appends the results as tool messages, and control goes back to the model. I also set a recursion limit when invoking the graph so a confused model can't loop forever.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Writing the loop as an explicit graph, rather than hiding it inside an agent class, paid off quickly. I could see every transition, test &lt;code&gt;should_continue&lt;/code&gt; with plain unit tests, and add nodes later (a query-rewriting step, for example) without touching the rest.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Tools: retriever and calculator
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The retriever tool's docstring matters as much as its code, because the model reads it to decide when to call it:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nd"&gt;@tool&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;retrieve_docs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;query&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;str&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;str&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"""&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Search the private knowledge base. Use for any question about
    internal documents. Returns the most relevant passages with sources.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"""&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;hits&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;retriever&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;search&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;query&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;k&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;settings&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;top_k&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="se"&gt;\n\n&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;join&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sa"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;h&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;source&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;] &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;h&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;h&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="ow"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;hits&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Returning the source with each passage lets the model cite where an answer came from, and makes bad retrievals easy to spot while debugging.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The calculator is deliberately narrow. It evaluates arithmetic expressions with a small safe parser rather than Python's &lt;code&gt;eval&lt;/code&gt;, because tool inputs are model-generated text and should be treated as untrusted.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  FAISS: simple, local, fast to load
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a private knowledge base of modest size, FAISS is a good fit: no separate database to run, and the index is just a file. I build the index offline (chunk documents, embed them, write the index plus a metadata file mapping vector IDs to text and source), then ship it with the application.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On Lambda that has a nice property: the index loads from the container's filesystem at cold start and stays in memory for warm invocations. I load it once at module level, not per request, so warm requests skip that cost.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The trade-off is that updating the knowledge base means rebuilding the index and redeploying. For a knowledge base that changes rarely, I'll take that over running a vector database.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  A provider-agnostic LLM layer
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I didn't want the agent tied to one vendor. The &lt;code&gt;llm&lt;/code&gt; module exposes one factory that returns a chat model supporting tool binding, chosen by config:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;get_llm&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;settings&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Settings&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;settings&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;provider&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;openai&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ChatOpenAI&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;model&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;settings&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;model&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;temperature&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;settings&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;provider&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;anthropic&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ChatAnthropic&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;model&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;settings&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;model&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;temperature&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;raise&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ValueError&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sa"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Unknown provider: &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;settings&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;provider&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The rest of the code depends only on this interface. Switching providers is a config change, which also makes it easy to compare how different models behave with the same tools and prompts. API keys come from the environment and never live in the code or the image.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  FastAPI on Lambda
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;FastAPI gives a typed request and response schema and automatic docs during local development. To run the same app on Lambda, I wrap it with an ASGI-to-Lambda adapter such as Mangum, so one codebase serves both &lt;code&gt;uvicorn&lt;/code&gt; locally and Lambda in production.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;app&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;FastAPI&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;class&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Ask&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;BaseModel&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;question&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;str&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nd"&gt;@app.post&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;/ask&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;ask&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Ask&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;app_graph&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;invoke&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;messages&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;user&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;question&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)]},&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;config&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;recursion_limit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;10&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;answer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;messages&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;][&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;].&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;content&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;handler&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Mangum&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;app&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


  Packaging as a Lambda container image
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Lambda's zip packages have tight size limits, and FAISS plus the LLM SDKs add up. A container image avoids that. The Dockerfile starts from the AWS Lambda Python base image, installs pinned dependencies, copies the code and the FAISS index, and points the command at the handler:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight docker"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;FROM&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt; public.ecr.aws/lambda/python:3.11&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;COPY&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt; requirements.txt .&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;RUN &lt;/span&gt;pip &lt;span class="nb"&gt;install&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--no-cache-dir&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-r&lt;/span&gt; requirements.txt
&lt;span class="k"&gt;COPY&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt; app/ ${LAMBDA_TASK_ROOT}/app/&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;COPY&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt; index/ ${LAMBDA_TASK_ROOT}/index/&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;CMD&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt; ["app.api.handler"]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;I can run the same image locally with Lambda's runtime interface emulator, which caught packaging mistakes before they reached AWS.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Terraform: zero manual setup
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The goal was that a fresh AWS account could go from nothing to a working HTTPS endpoint with &lt;code&gt;terraform apply&lt;/code&gt;. The configuration provisions:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;an &lt;strong&gt;ECR repository&lt;/strong&gt; for the image;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;an &lt;strong&gt;IAM role&lt;/strong&gt; for the function with only the permissions it needs, including logging;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;the &lt;strong&gt;Lambda function&lt;/strong&gt; using the image, with memory, timeout, and environment variables (provider, model, top-k) set in code;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;an &lt;strong&gt;HTTPS endpoint&lt;/strong&gt; in front of the function.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight hcl"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;resource&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"aws_lambda_function"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"agent"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nx"&gt;function_name&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"agentic-rag"&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nx"&gt;package_type&lt;/span&gt;  &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Image"&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nx"&gt;image_uri&lt;/span&gt;     &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"${aws_ecr_repository.agent.repository_url}:${var.image_tag}"&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nx"&gt;role&lt;/span&gt;          &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;aws_iam_role&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;lambda&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;arn&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nx"&gt;timeout&lt;/span&gt;       &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;60&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nx"&gt;memory_size&lt;/span&gt;   &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1024&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nx"&gt;environment&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nx"&gt;variables&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;LLM_PROVIDER&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;var&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;llm_provider&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Two settings deserve attention. The &lt;strong&gt;timeout&lt;/strong&gt; has to cover several LLM round trips, because an agentic question can take more than one model call. &lt;strong&gt;Memory&lt;/strong&gt; on Lambda also scales CPU, which affects how quickly the FAISS index loads. I treat both as variables and tune them rather than hard-coding guesses. Secrets such as API keys should come from a secrets service, not plain Terraform variables in version control.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Having everything in Terraform also makes teardown trivial, which matters for a side project you don't want billing you quietly.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Testing
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Because each component is separate, most tests don't need an LLM or AWS:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;the retriever is tested against a tiny fixture index;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;the calculator is tested on valid and malicious inputs;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;the routing function is tested with hand-built messages that do and don't contain tool calls;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;the graph is tested end-to-end with a fake LLM that returns scripted tool calls.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That last test is the most useful: it checks the wiring of the loop deterministically, without paying for or depending on a real model.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What I learned
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Tool descriptions are prompts.&lt;/strong&gt; Vague docstrings led to the model skipping retrieval or over-calling it. Clear "use this when" language fixed most routing problems.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Make the loop explicit.&lt;/strong&gt; An explicit graph is easier to debug and extend than an opaque agent executor.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Bound everything.&lt;/strong&gt; Recursion limits, Lambda timeouts, and a narrow calculator keep an autonomous loop predictable.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Infrastructure as code from day one.&lt;/strong&gt; It was more work up front than clicking through the console, and it was worth it the first time I rebuilt the stack.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What's next
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'd like to add a query-rewriting node before retrieval, evaluation on a fixed question set to compare providers and chunking strategies, and response streaming. If you're building something similar, I'd be glad to compare notes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Code and setup details: &lt;a href="https://github.com/Ved210105" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;github.com/Ved210105&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
